<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>DW - English for Africa</title><subtitle type="html">News, Analysis and Service from Germany and Europe - in 30 Languages</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-africa</id><updated>2026-09-09T04:54:09Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss-en-africa" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-12756?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-africa" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Kenya: Crackdown on foreign traders prompts xenophobia fears</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:dc7764dd2d6ce9dfe1c1924c69c405902e74318021066e491dbc2ebef8552fdb</id><updated>2026-09-09T04:54:09Z</updated><published>2026-09-08T09:14:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/kenya-crackdown-on-foreign-traders-prompts-xenophobia-fears/a-79143847--a8e571d0a365.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/kenya-crackdown-on-foreign-traders-prompts-xenophobia-fears/a-79143847?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Concerns about xenophobia are rising in Kenya after President William Ruto ordered a crackdown on foreigners working as hawkers. The government insists however that legally documented foreign traders remain protected.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Morocco earthquake 3 years on: 'No one has remembered us'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:798b99283bf1d0ad64de99a80dd603d18b75456994127e5ddebb7d594a8a9877</id><updated>2026-09-09T04:54:09Z</updated><published>2026-09-07T18:57:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/morocco-earthquake-3-years-on-no-one-has-remembered-us/a-78874844--cbd4f3eb032b.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/morocco-earthquake-3-years-on-no-one-has-remembered-us/a-78874844?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Morocco has rebuilt thousands of homes since the 2023 earthquake. But some survivors are still living in tents and say they have received no state aid.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>How climate change and food insecurity could fuel future conflicts</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e31380a422208f75e891baa1c3061c9322499de1dd9686ee8f3abb65398110d9</id><updated>2026-09-09T04:54:09Z</updated><published>2026-09-06T07:47:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/how-climate-change-and-food-insecurity-could-fuel-future-conflicts/a-78871431--8e63f64c4115.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/how-climate-change-and-food-insecurity-could-fuel-future-conflicts/a-78871431?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Climate change, conflict, and trade tensions are putting pressure on the global food system. New research suggests the biggest risk is not food shortages, but whether societies can peacefully adapt to change.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Ebola: Why children are last to be treated in medical crises</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:661f0991b3eecf5b8362f9d7b219ad8259cbd0b590765614a1b8751efdddd56c</id><updated>2026-09-09T04:54:09Z</updated><published>2026-09-06T06:13:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/ebola-why-children-are-last-to-be-treated-in-medical-crises/a-79050107--c059b44b485e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/ebola-why-children-are-last-to-be-treated-in-medical-crises/a-79050107?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">In the latest Ebola outbreak in the Democratic Republic of Congo, children are more likely to die than adults. But it's not always due to the disease. Specific medicines for younger people are often harder to come by.</summary><category term="World" /></entry></feed>
